  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 AM EDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nCurrently, Hurricane Isaias is located off the Yucatan Peninsula. \nLocally, high pressure remains in place with a subtropical jet to \nour south. Initially, this will keep the region dry and mild with \nIsaias expected to continue northeast through the day before \nshifting north late tonight. Throughout the day on Friday, high \npressure will shift more to our northeast with height rises keeping \ntemperatures well into the 80s. The subtropical jet will also shift \nnorth as Isaias makes landfall along the northern Gulf by Saturday \nmorning. \n\nThere still remains uncertainty in the exact track after landfall \nwith a progression ranging from the Mississippi River Valley to \nMiddle Tennessee possible by Saturday night. Initially, this will \nlead to an increase in rain chances from the south and increasing \neasterly and northeasterly flow that shifts southeasterly by the \nevening. Regardless of the exact track, strong winds in the higher \nelevations remains the largest impact for our region, especially in \nthe mountains and foothills. With the strength of the MSLP gradient \nand low-level flow, advisory-level winds are also increasingly \nlikely across portions of the Cumberland Plateau. However, warning-\nlevel winds are likely in the mountains and foothills as any \nsolution has 850mb flow in excess of 60 kts to our west. The \nstrength of downsloping will also limit overall rain totals for the \neastern half of the area until flow becomes more westerly by Sunday \nmorning. Locally heavy rainfall and isolated flooding are possible, \nmainly in southern and western portions of the area.  \n\nFor the severe threat, current trends continue to suggest \ninstability will stay largely to our south and west, especially if a \nmore eastward track transpires after landfall. However, with the \nstrength of 850mb winds, some damaging winds could possibly reach \nthe surface in southern portions or the Plateau. Otherwise, most \nwinds will be due to downsloping or the MSLP gradient. By Sunday \nevening into Sunday night, the remnants will continue to weaken as \nthey progress off to our northeast. High pressure will then build in \nand give way to drier conditions into next week with above normal \n500mb heights keeping temperatures mild.",
